ption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about biological sequence generation and evaluation.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- Panel **a**: Workflow schematic with three rows labeled **Unconditional Generation**, **Species-specific Generation**, and **Downstream Task Generation**. Columns are **Prompt**, **Generated Metadata**, and **Generated Sequence**. Prompts include examples such as RNA, Homo sapiens, and histone H1 with special tokens. Generated outputs show metadata text followed by RNA/protein-like generated sequences with colored highlighted segments and special tokens.\n\n- Panels **b** and **c**: Scatter plots comparing semantic similarity against sequence quality scores. Points are colored in a viridis-like gradient, with marginal histograms on top and right. Panel b reports a **Spearman ρ ≈ 0.32** and **macro similarity ≈ 0.72**. Panel c reports **Spearman ρ ≈ 0.43** and **mean similarity ≈ 0.70**. X axes appear to be model/sequence-quality scores; y axes are semantic similarity.\n\n- Panel **d**: Density plots comparing **Real Genomes** and **Generated Sequences**. Two subplots show distributions for **CDS GC3 content (%)** and **CDS GC content (%)**. Generated sequences are shown in orange and real genomes in dark blue/gray, with generated distributions shifted higher.\n\n- Panel **e**: Boxplots comparing **generated** versus **real genome** values across organisms: **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. Metrics shown include **CDS U/C content (%)** and **CDS GC3 content (%)**. Yellow star markers indicate real-genome values overlaid on generated distributions.\n\n- Panel **f**: Circular/radar-style plots for the same organisms: **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. Each compares **Real** versus **Generated** profiles using organism-specific colors.\n\n- Panel **g**: Bar charts comparing amino-acid frequencies between **generated** and **real genome** sequences for **Human**, **Zebrafish**, **Fruit fly**, **Arabidopsis**, **Yeast**, and **Maize**. X axes list amino-acid one-letter codes; y axes are frequency.\n\nBiological source objects visible: RNA sequences, coding sequences/CDS, genomes, protein or amino-acid sequences, and species-specific genomes from human, zebrafish, fruit fly, Arabidopsis, yeast, and maize.\n\nOverall visible finding: generated biological sequences are evaluated against real genomes using semantic similarity, sequence-quality scores, GC/GC3 composition, codon or nucleotide-content distributions, circular composition profiles, and amino-acid frequency comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 30, "caption": "Fig. 4: Controllable generation of structured non-coding RNAs and protein-coding sequences", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-h**, showing analyses of generated biological macromolecular/RNA sequence or structure outputs compared with natural/reference data.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Scatter/strip plot of **gRNA-miRNA-seq score**, grouped by categories including **Exact match**, **wobble pairing**, **isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and likely **No detection**. Horizontal reference lines are shown. Points are color-coded by category.\n- **Panel b:** Stacked bar chart showing **percentage (%)** outcomes across amino-acid/tRNA-like labels. Legend categories include **Exact match**, **Wobble pairing**, **Isoacceptor**, **Incorrect isotype**, and **No detection**.\n- **Panel c:** Histogram/density plot of **sequence length (%)**, comparing **Class II (all tRNAs)** vs **Class I (tRNAs)** or similar class labels.\n- **Panel d:** Violin plots comparing **Real** vs **Generated** distributions for internal bit scores across RNA families: **5S**, **5.8S**, **16S**, and **28S**.\n- **Panel e:** Multiple scatter plots of **Isoelectric Point (pI)** vs **Molecular Weight (kDa)** for **natural sequences** and generated sequences under different **temperature / Top-p** sampling settings. Points are colored by **histone type**, with labels such as **H1**, **H2A**, **H2B**, **H3**, and **H4**.\n- **Panel f:** Boxplots of **ProGen2 perplexity** grouped by **histone type**, comparing natural sequences and generated outputs under several sampling conditions.\n- **Panel g:** Predicted protein/disordered-region visualization with labeled **N-term**, **C-term**, **Intrinsic Disordered Regions**, and **Globular Domain**. A confidence color scale labeled **pLDDT confidence** ranges roughly from low red/orange to high blue.\n- **Panel h:** Four circular/radial structural or sequence maps comparing **Natural Sequence** and **Generated** traces under different temperature/top-p sampling settings.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied: **tRNAs or tRNA-like sequences**, **ribosomal RNA families 5S/5.8S/16S/28S**, and **histone proteins H1/H2A/H2B/H3/H4**.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces visible: comparison of **real/natural vs generated sequences**, generated under different **sampling parameters** such as **temperature** and **Top-p**.\n\nLikely findings shown visually: generated sequences are being evaluated against natural biological sequences by matching class, sequence length, covariance/internal bit score distributions, protein physicochemical properties, perplexity, predicted structure confidence, disorder regions, and circular structural/sequence representations."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 32, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a–h** describing a multimodal nucleotide sequence foundation model and tokenizer analysis.\n\nPanel **a** shows a workflow for **multi-modal nucleotide sequences**. Inputs include **NCBI RefSeq GenBank files** with plasmids/genomes/chromosomes, plus hybrid nucleotide language materials: **genomic DNA and RNA**, **natural language annotations**, and **XML structural tags**. These are processed by a **cross-modal BPE tokenizer** with **6,000 vocabulary size** and used to train a **GenNA Foundation Model**, described as a **Qwen3 decoder-only transformer with 3.6B parameters**. The model interface is shown for causal language modeling / next-token prediction over mixed biological and annotation tokens. Output capabilities include sequence-function alignment, understanding biological rules, zero-shot variant effect prediction, species-specific genomic fingerprints, latent-space clustering, phylogenetic tree reconstruction, unconditional self-guided generation, targeted ncRNA generation, and protein-coding sequence generation.\n\nPanel **b** is a pie chart showing source composition: **RNA 73.1%** and **genomic DNA 26.9%**.\n\nPanel **c** is a length distribution histogram comparing **genomic DNA** and **RNA** sequence lengths in characters. Both distributions are concentrated at shorter lengths with a long tail extending toward 20,000 characters.\n\nPanel **d** contains two taxonomic composition pie charts. Visible groups include **plant**, **invertebrate**, **vertebrate mammalian**, **vertebrate other**, **fungi**, and **protozoa**, with vertebrate other and plant comprising large portions.\n\nPanel **e** illustrates cross-modal BPE tokenization of a multimodal input sequence containing a tRNA annotation and nucleotide sequence, e.g. `...tRNA-Glu<seq>ATAC<gene><tRNA>TATTT...`. The sequence is transformed into a **BPE token sequence**, then mapped into **token IDs**.\n\nPanel **f** is a donut chart of tokenizer vocabulary composition for **vocabulary 6000**, broken down by token type and count. It includes nucleotide k-mers from **1-mer** through **9-mer and above**, plus **N-containing kmers** and **non-nucleotide** tokens. The largest visible segment is **7-mer**, followed by **8-mer** and **6-mer** categories.\n\nPanel **g** is a bar chart of **coverage by k-mer length**, showing decreasing coverage as k-mer 